{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789460853,"nonce":"NniYytW2dV2oYpvl+yHObHCLMWI=","title":"bench_runner: zypy rows run in one process, the interpreter rows in fresh ones","message":"`--runs N` runs the zypy kernel N times in the runner's process, one runtime after another, and takes the median; the CPython, PyPy and uv rows are N fresh processes. The Zyntax runs share the pool and the collector: the previous run's live set is garbage the next run's first collection retires, the heap counter carries over, and where a collection lands relative to the kernel's phases shifts from run to run. On bench_pipeline the in-process exec reads anywhere from 53 to 120 ms across invocations while `zypy run` standalone is 60 ms every time.\n\nFix: run each zypy repetition as a child `zypy run` like the interpreters (setup, compile and exec split can come back over a pipe, or `--trace-phases` on stderr), so the rows measure the same thing. Until then read a zypy row against its standalone time before publishing it.","files":null}]}